description:
Write a `Buffer` of ints that owns a raw `new[]` array and gets copying and moving right:

```cpp
class Buffer
{
public:
    Buffer();
    explicit Buffer(std::size_t size, int fill = 0);
    ~Buffer();
    Buffer(const Buffer& other);
    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept;
    Buffer& operator=(Buffer other) noexcept;
    friend void swap(Buffer& a, Buffer& b) noexcept;

    std::size_t size() const;
    const int* data() const;
    int& operator[](std::size_t i);
    int operator[](std::size_t i) const;
};
```

- The default constructor makes an empty buffer (`size()` 0, `data()` `nullptr`). The other allocates `size` ints with `new int[]`, all set to `fill`; a size of 0 allocates nothing.
- The **copy constructor** makes a deep copy: its own array with the same values.
- The **move constructor** takes the other buffer's array and leaves the other one empty.
- **Assignment** uses copy-and-swap: take the parameter by value and swap with it.
- The destructor frees the array with `delete[]`.

The tests count `new[]` and `delete[]` calls, so a shallow copy, a leak or a double free shows up.

